## Network Inspection and Troubleshooting Tools

Low-level commands allow administrators to inspect active routing tables, socket connections, and interface details directly from the kernel.

```
[ Network Error ] ➔ [ ip addr / ip route ] ➔ [ ping / ss ] ➔ [ Identify Bottleneck ]

```

### Diagnostic Utilities

* `ip addr show` displays active IP addresses, subnet masks, and interface states.
* `ip route show` prints the kernel routing table to verify default gateways and network paths.
* `ping -c 4 hostname_or_ip` tests network connectivity to a remote host using ICMP echo requests.
* `ss -tuln` lists all active listening TCP and UDP sockets along with their port numbers.
* `host hostname` or `dig hostname` tests DNS resolution by querying configured domain name servers.

---

## Hostnames and Name Resolution

Hostnames uniquely identify systems on a network, while name resolution translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses.

```
[ DNS Query ] ➔ [ Check /etc/hosts ] ➔ [ Query DNS via /etc/resolv.conf ] ➔ [ IP Resolved ]

```

### Configuration Files and Tools

* `hostnamectl set-hostname server1.example.com` updates the static system hostname instantly across reboots.
* `/etc/hosts` provides local, static IP-to-hostname mappings that take priority over external DNS queries.
* `/etc/resolv.conf` defines active DNS nameservers automatically generated by NetworkManager connection profiles.